Novel BMI Locus										References						
PAX3 (rs994108): 	Encodes a transcription factor that aids in converting white to brown-like adipocyte progenitors.  Common variation in PAX3 is involved in craniofacial morphologies 1 (Paternoster et al., 2012) and severe mutations in this gene cause deformations, deafness and pigmentation changes involved in Waardenburg syndrome types 1 and 3 (Sommer et al., 1983; Read and Newton, 1997; Wollnik et al., 2003). Somatic mutations in PAX3 have been associated with rhabdomyosarcoma (Anderson et al., 1999; Anderson et al., 2001).  In adults, PAX3 is most expressed in adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, skin, arteries, mammary tissue, testes, and some regions of the brain (GTEx release v8, https://gtexportal.org/home/). 									Paternoster, L., Zhurov, A.I., Toma, A.M., Kemp, J.P., St Pourcain, B., Timpson, N.J., McMahon, G., McArdle, W., Ring, S.M., Smith, G.D., et al. (2012). Genome-wide association study of three-dimensional facial morphology identifies a variant in PAX3 associated with nasion position. American journal of human genetics 90, 478-485.	Sommer, A., Young-Wee, T., and Frye, T. (1983). Previously undescribed syndrome of craniofacial, hand anomalies, and sensorineural deafness. Am J Med Genet 15, 71-77.	Read, A.P., and Newton, V.E. (1997). Waardenburg syndrome. J Med Genet 34, 656-665.	Wollnik B, Tukel T, Uyguner O, Ghanbari A, Kayserili H, Emiroglu M, Yuksel-Apak M. Homozygous and heterozygous inheritance of PAX3 mutations causes different types of Waardenburg syndrome. Am J Med Genet A. 2003 Sep 15;122A(1):42-5	Anderson J, Gordon A, Pritchard-Jones K, Shipley J. Genes, chromosomes, and rhabdomyosarcoma. Genes Chromosomes Cancer. 1999 Dec;26(4):275-85. Review	Anderson J, Ramsay A, Gould S, Pritchard-Jones K. PAX3-FKHR induces morphological change and enhances cellular proliferation and invasion in rhabdomyosarcoma. Am J Pathol. 2001 Sep;159(3):1089-96. 	
Novel Findings in Known BMI Loci										References						
ADCY5 (rs17361324): 	Adenylyl cyclases mediate G protein-coupled receptor signaling through the synthesis of the second messenger cAMP.  SNPs in this gene have been associated with type 2 diabetes (Dupuis et al., 2010; Zhao et al., 2017) and adverse birth outcomes (Freathy et al., 2010; Zhang et al., 2017) both of which are related to obesity. These previously associated SNPs range in their correlation with our lead SNP (r² in AMR ranging from 0.0025 to 0.7229; (Machiela and Chanock, 2015)).  This gene has also been associated with ADCY5-related dyskinesia, a pediatric onset movement disorder involving the limbs, neck, or face (Shaw et al., 2014). ADCY5 is most expressed in arterial tissue, heart, brain and esophagus (GTEx), known to have various isoforms (O'Leary et al., 2016). The lead ADCY5 SNP from this study, rs17361324, is proximate to an ADCY5 intronic variant (rs1093467, r²=0.3 in 1000 Genomes AMR) that is highly conserved (Haploreg v4.1, https://pubs.broadinstitute.org/mammals/haploreg/haploreg.php). 									Dupuis, J., Langenberg, C., Prokopenko, I., Saxena, R., Soranzo, N., Jackson, A.U., Wheeler, E., Glazer, N.L., Bouatia-Naji, N., Gloyn, A.L., et al. (2010). New genetic loci implicated in fasting glucose homeostasis and their impact on type 2 diabetes risk. Nat Genet 42, 105-116.	Zhao, W., Rasheed, A., Tikkanen, E., Lee, J.J., Butterworth, A.S., Howson, J.M.M., Assimes, T.L., Chowdhury, R., Orho-Melander, M., Damrauer, S., et al. (2017). Identification of new susceptibility loci for type 2 diabetes and shared etiological pathways with coronary heart disease. Nat Genet 49, 1450-1457.	Freathy, R.M., Mook-Kanamori, D.O., Sovio, U., Prokopenko, I., Timpson, N.J., Berry, D.J., Warrington, N.M., Widen, E., Hottenga, J.J., Kaakinen, M., et al. (2010). Variants in ADCY5 and near CCNL1 are associated with fetal growth and birth weight. Nat Genet 42, 430-435.	Zhang, G., Feenstra, B., Bacelis, J., Liu, X., Muglia, L.M., Juodakis, J., Miller, D.E., Litterman, N., Jiang, P.P., Russell, L., et al. (2017). Genetic Associations with Gestational Duration and Spontaneous Preterm Birth. N Engl J Med 377, 1156-1167.	Shaw C, Hisama F, Friedman J, et al. ADCY5-Related Dyskinesia. 2014 Dec 18 [Updated 2015 Dec 17]. In: Adam MP, Ardinger HH, Pagon RA, et al., editors. GeneReviews® [Internet]. Seattle (WA): University of Washington, Seattle; 1993-2019. Available from: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK263441/	Machiela MJ, Chanock SJ. LDlink a web-based application for exploring population-specific haplotype structure and linking correlated alleles of possible functional variants. Bioinformatics. 2015 Jul 2. PMID: 26139635.	O'Leary NA, Wright MW, Brister JR, Ciufo S, Haddad D, McVeigh R, Rajput B, Robbertse B, Smith-White B, Ako-Adjei D, Astashyn A, Badretdin A, Bao Y, Blinkova O, Brover V, Chetvernin V, Choi J, Cox E, Ermolaeva O, Farrell CM, Goldfarb T, Gupta T, Haft D, Hatcher E, Hlavina W, Joardar VS, Kodali VK, Li W, Maglott D, Masterson P, McGarvey KM, Murphy MR, O'Neill K, Pujar S, Rangwala SH, Rausch D, Riddick LD, Schoch C, Shkeda A, Storz SS, Sun H, Thibaud-Nissen F, Tolstoy I, Tully RE, Vatsan AR, Wallin C, Webb D, Wu W, Landrum MJ, Kimchi A, Tatusova T, DiCuccio M, Kitts P, Murphy TD, Pruitt KD. Reference sequence (RefSeq) database at NCBI: current status, taxonomic expansion, and functional annotation. Nucleic Acids Res. 2016 Jan 4;44(D1):D733-45
ILRUN (rs148899910): 	ILRUN encodes an inhibitor of the innate antiviral response (Ambrose et al., 2018). ILRUN has also been associated with multipe height phenotypes (Soranzo et al., 2009; Weedon et al., 2008; Cousminer et al., 2013), including one report with female stature in Korean women at the same lead SNP as in our analyses (Cho et al., 2021).  The first of two BMI signals described at ILRUN was at index SNP, rs205262, an eQTL for another gene within the region, SNRPC, in European ancestry samples (Locke et al., 2015); the second signal (rs75398113) has also been reported at SNRPC for extremes of the body mass index distribution (Riveros-McKay et al., 2019). Yet, our novel signal, led by rs148899910, is in low LD with these two index SNPs (r2=0.01-0.05 in AMR), and was in high LD (r2>0.8 in 1000 Genomes AMR) with several intergenic variants, and intergenic/intronic for SMIM29 and HMGA1 variants, several of which appear to bind several proteins and have promoter/enhancer histone marks (Haploreg).									Ambrose, R. L., Liu, Y. C., Adams, T. E., Bean, A., & Stewart, C. R. (2018). C6orf106 is a novel inhibitor of the interferon-regulatory factor 3-dependent innate antiviral response. The Journal of biological chemistry, 293(27), 10561–10573. doi:10.1074/jbc.RA117.001491	Soranzo, N., Rivadeneira, F., Chinappen-Horsley, U., Malkina, I., Richards, J.B., Hammond, N., Stolk, L., Nica, A., Inouye, M., Hofman, A., et al. (2009). Meta-analysis of genome-wide scans for human adult stature identifies novel Loci and associations with measures of skeletal frame size. PLoS Genet 5, e1000445.	Weedon, M.N., Lango, H., Lindgren, C.M., Wallace, C., Evans, D.M., Mangino, M., Freathy, R.M., Perry, J.R., Stevens, S., Hall, A.S., et al. (2008). Genome-wide association analysis identifies 20 loci that influence adult height. Nat Genet 40, 575-583.	Cousminer, D.L., Berry, D.J., Timpson, N.J., Ang, W., Thiering, E., Byrne, E.M., Taal, H.R., Huikari, V., Bradfield, J.P., Kerkhof, M., et al. (2013). Genome-wide association and longitudinal analyses reveal genetic loci linking pubertal height growth, pubertal timing and childhood adiposity. Hum Mol Genet 22, 2735-2747.	Cho, H.W., Jin, H.S., and Eom, Y.B. (2021). A Genome-Wide Association Study of Novel Genetic Variants Associated With Anthropometric Traits in Koreans. Front Genet 12, 669215.	Locke, A.E., Kahali, B., Berndt, S.I., Justice, A.E., Pers, T.H., Day, F.R., Powell, C., Vedantam, S., Buchkovich, M.L., Yang, J., et al. (2015). Genetic studies of body mass index yield new insights for obesity biology. Nature 518, 197-206.	Riveros-McKay, F., Mistry, V., Bounds, R., Hendricks, A., Keogh, J.M., Thomas, H., Henning, E., Corbin, L.J., Understanding Society Scientific, G., O'Rahilly, S., et al. (2019). Genetic architecture of human thinness compared to severe obesity. PLoS Genet 15, e1007603.
Novel Findings in a Known Height Locus										References						
B4GALNT3 (rs215226)	Transfers N-acetylgalactosamine to form unique structure of N-glycans on cell surface (Ikehara et al., 2006) and is most expressed in the stomach (GTEx).  The lead SNP for our signal is in an intronic region of B4GALNT3 and and appears to have several promoter and enhancer histone marks (Haploreg).  Three other variants in this gene (r2 0.2395 to 0.7938 in 1000 Genomes AMR) have been associated with body height and reported in the NHGRI-EBI GWAS Catalog (Kichaev et al., 2019; Tachmazidou et al., 2017; Wood et al., 2014; Akiyama et al., 2019; Buniello et al., 2019).									Ikehara, Y., Sato, T., Niwa, T., Nakamura, S., Gotoh, M., Ikehara, S.K., Kiyohara, K., Aoki, C., Iwai, T., Nakanishi, H., Hirabayashi, J., Tatematsu, M., & Narimatsu, H. (2006). Apical Golgi localization of N,N'-diacetyllactosediamine synthase, beta4GalNAc-T3, is responsible for LacdiNAc expression on gastric mucosa. Glycobiology, 16 (9), 777-85. 	Kichaev, G., Bhatia, G., Loh, P.R., Gazal, S., Burch, K., Freund, M.K., Schoech, A., Pasaniuc, B., Price, A.L. (2019). Leveraging polygenic functional enrichment to improve GWAS power. Am J Hum Genet 104 (1), 65-75.	Tachmazidou, I., Süveges, D., Min, J.L., Ritchie, G.R.S., Steinberg, J., Walter, K., Iotchkova, V., Schwartzentruber, J., Huang, J., Memari, Y., et al. (2017). Whole-genome sequencing coupled to imputation discovers genetic signals for anthropometric traits. Am J Hum Genet. 100(6), 865-884.	Wood, A. R., Esko, T., Yang, J., Vedantam, S., Pers, T.H., Gustafsson, S., Chu, A.Y., Estrada, K., Luan, J., Kutalik, Z., et al. (2014) Defining the role of common variation in the genomic and biological architecture of adult human height. Nat Genet. 46(11), 1173-86.	Akiyama, M., Ishigaki, K., Sakaue, S., Momozawa, Y., Horikoshi, M., Hirata, M., Matsuda, K., Ikegawa, S., Takahashi, A., Kanai, M., et al. (2019). Characterizing rare and low-frequency height-associated variants in the Japanese population. Nat Commun. 10(1), 4393.	Buniello, A., MacArthur, J.A.L., Cerezo, M., Harris, L.W., Hayhurst, J., Malangone, C., McMahon, A., Morales, J., Mountjoy, E., Sollis, E.,  et al. (2019). The NHGRI-EBI GWAS Catalog of published genome-wide association studies, targeted arrays and summary statistics 2019. Nucleic Acids Research, 47 (Database issue): D1005-D1012.	
